I am trying to identify an operation that takes two matrices of dimensions $n^2$ and output a matrix of dimensions $n^2$.
Every element in the output matrix incorporates all elements from the input matrices.
All possible multiplication pairs from elements of the input matrices appear only once throughout the output matrix. So individual output elements have all distinct pairs.
Here is an example given the matrices:
$$ A = \left[\begin{array}{cc} 
a_{11} &a_{12} \\ 
a_{21} &a_{22} 
\end{array}\right] \;\; , \;\; 
B = \left[\begin{array}{cc} 
b_{11} &b_{12} \\ 
b_{21} &b_{22} 
\end{array}\right]$$
What is the operation that produce:
$$operation(A,B) = \left[\begin{array} \\ 
a_{11}b_{11}+a_{12}b_{21}+a_{22}b_{12}+a_{21}b_{22} & a_{11}b_{12}+a_{12}b_{22}+a_{22}b_{11}+a_{21}b_{21} \\
a_{21}b_{11}+a_{22}b_{21}+a_{12}b_{12}+a_{11}b_{22} & a_{21}b_{12}+a_{22}b_{22}+a_{12}b_{11}+a_{11}b_{21}
\end{array}\right]$$
I find out that it is equivalent to these matrix multiplications;
$$operation(A,B) = AB + C_{up} A C_{up} C_{left} B$$
Where $C_{up}$ is a up Cyclic permutation and $C_{left}$ a left one.
The second term is basically the following matrix multiplication:
$$\left[\begin{matrix}
a_{21} & a_{22} \\
a_{11} & a_{12}
\end{matrix}\right]\left[\begin{matrix}
b_{22} & b_{21} \\
b_{12} & b_{11}
\end{matrix}\right]$$
If the matrices are 3x3 instead of 2x2, the result become:
$$operation(A,B) = AB + C_{up}A C_{up} C_{left} B + C_{down} A C_{down} C_{right} B$$
Does it relate to a standard tensor operation?

